# Copy these into wherever this worker actually runs (GitHub Actions
# repo secrets, a Render worker's Environment, or a VM's .env).

# TiDB Cloud Starter connection string(s). Moved off Prisma Postgres
# entirely — Prisma's 2GB/workspace free-tier cap was already being
# exceeded by the live data, and scaling it further meant more separate
# email sign-ups, which ran out. TiDB Cloud Starter gives up to 5 free
# instances PER ACCOUNT (no card needed), 5GiB storage + 50M request
# units/month each — 25GB total under one login. This worker shards
# across as many free instances as you give it: set DATABASE_URL_1,
# DATABASE_URL_2, DATABASE_URL_3, ... (any count — add more any time
# BEFORE you start a pass, see scan_worker.py's module docstring for why
# the count has to stay fixed mid-pass) and it splits the full tile list
# evenly across them, scanning all of them AT THE SAME TIME. Apply
# database/schema.mysql.sql to EVERY shard database by hand before wiring
# up its secret — same one-time manual step as a single-database setup,
# just repeated once per shard.
#
# Get each one's connection string from TiDB Cloud's dashboard, "Connect"
# panel — it looks like mysql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:4000/DBNAME. TiDB Cloud
# requires TLS (handled automatically by scan_worker.py's connection code
# unless the URL has a trailing ?ssl=false, which is only for a local,
# SSL-less MySQL/MariaDB test instance).
#
# NEED MORE THAN 5 FREE INSTANCES TOTAL (i.e. more than one TiDB account)?
# You don't need a new real inbox per account. Gmail/Outlook/most mail
# providers treat "+tag" as part of the address but still deliver to your
# one real inbox — name@gmail.com, name+shard2@gmail.com, name+shard3@
# gmail.com, etc. all land in the same place, but sign-up forms (including
# TiDB Cloud's) see them as distinct, valid email addresses. Use a new
# +tag per account to open as many 5-instance/25GB TiDB accounts as the
# project ever needs, with zero new inboxes. (This is why TiDB — not
# Layerbase — won for this project: TiDB has no idle-sleep policy and no
# anti-keep-alive detection, so it's safe to run as the live production
# database; Layerbase's own policy explicitly puts a database back to
# sleep if it detects a poller/keep-alive ping keeping it awake, and its
# free tier is scoped to dev/test/eval, not a revenue-generating product.)
DATABASE_URL_1=mysql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:4000/DBNAME
# DATABASE_URL_2=mysql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:4000/DBNAME
# DATABASE_URL_3=mysql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:4000/DBNAME

# Only have one database? A bare DATABASE_URL (no numeric suffix) still
# works as a single implicit shard — you don't need the _1 at all for a
# one-database setup:
# DATABASE_URL=mysql://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:4000/DBNAME

# Tuning — defaults below are sized for Render's base 512MB worker tier.
# A fixed TILE_DEG can never be "safe everywhere" on its own (a 2-degree
# box over open ocean vs. over a dense US metro are wildly different
# workloads), so any region that still runs out of memory at this size
# gets automatically quartered and retried down to MIN_SPLIT_DEG — that's
# the real fix for OOMs, these knobs are just the starting point.
# If you're on GitHub Actions (7GB+ runners) or a bigger plan, raise
# DUCKDB_MEMORY_LIMIT / DUCKDB_THREADS / TILE_DEG for faster passes.
# IMPORTANT: shards now run concurrently (one thread per shard), so
# DUCKDB_MEMORY_LIMIT is PER SHARD and total usage is roughly
# (number of shards) x this value — lower it if you're running a lot of
# shards at once on a memory-capped host.
TILE_DEG=2
MIN_SPLIT_DEG=0.1
MAX_SPLIT_DEPTH=8
DUCKDB_THREADS=1
DUCKDB_MEMORY_LIMIT=300MB
MIN_CONFIDENCE=0.0
SCAN_IDLE_SLEEP_SECONDS=21600
INGEST_BATCH_SIZE=500

# Only set this on a bounded host (GitHub Actions) — unset on Render/a VM
# where the worker should just run forever.
# MAX_RUNTIME_SECONDS=19800
